			<div class="im-prose">
				<h2>Use it</h2>
				<p>Copy the folder into a Ghost theme — templates under <code>partials/</code>, the rest under <code>assets/</code> — and call it from any template:</p>
				{{{{code}}}}
partials/sections/stats-band/   ← stats.hbs + parts/
assets/sections/stats-band/     ← stats.css, stats.js

{{> "sections/stats-band/stats"
	caption="In numbers"  index="04"
	title="Three years of writing in public."
	text="Counted by Ghost itself."
	cta_label="Read the story"  cta_url="/about/"
	stat_1_value="12480"  stat_1_text="12,480"  stat_1_label="Readers"
	stat_1_delta="12% this month"  stat_1_spark="4,6,5,8,7,11,10,14,13,17"
	stat_2_value="41.2"  stat_2_suffix="%"  stat_2_label="Open rate"
	stat_2_delta="1.4 pts"  stat_2_trend="down"
	bars="3.1,3.4,4.2,4.0,5.1,5.8"  bar_labels="Jan,Feb,Mar,Apr,May,Jun"
	bars_caption="Readers by month, thousands"
}}
				{{{{/code}}}}
				<p>It loads its own stylesheet and script through the layout's <code>styles</code> and <code>scripts</code> blocks. Count-up and sparklines come from the system's <code>im-motion.js</code> and <code>im-charts.js</code>; without them the numbers simply stand still, which is a fine fallback.</p>

				<h2>Parameters</h2>
				<div class="im-table-scroll">
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th></th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>caption</code>, <code>index</code></td><td>The mono caption above the title, and its number.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>title</code>, <code>text</code></td><td>Heading and one paragraph.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cta_label</code>, <code>cta_url</code></td><td>A link under the text.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_value</code></td><td>N is 1–4. A plain number — <code>12480</code>, <code>41.2</code>. It is what gets counted up to; its decimals are kept.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_text</code></td><td>What to print before JavaScript runs — <code>12,480</code>. Defaults to the value.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_prefix</code>, <code>_suffix</code></td><td><code>$</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>k</code>.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_label</code></td><td>What it counts.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_delta</code>, <code>_trend</code></td><td>How it moved, and <code>up</code> (default), <code>down</code> or <code>flat</code>.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>stat_N_spark</code></td><td>Comma-separated values for a sparkline.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>bars</code>, <code>bar_labels</code>, <code>bars_caption</code>, <code>bars_on</code></td><td>The bar row: values, labels, caption, and which bar is lit (from 1; the last by default). Leave <code>bars</code> out and the row is gone.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
				</div>
				<p>Two figures or three work as well as four — leave the others out. For numbers from Ghost, pass them in: <code>stat_1_value=@site.members_count</code> is not something Ghost exposes, so most sites author these in the template or a custom setting.</p>

				<h2>How it is built</h2>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>The figures are the system's <a href="/components/stats/">stats components</a></strong> — <code>im-stat-figure</code>, <code>im-stat-delta</code>, <code>im-spark</code>, <code>im-bars</code>. The section's stylesheet is layout only, under <code>im-statband__*</code>.</li>
					<li><strong>Shared borders.</strong> The grid's background is the line colour and its gap is 1px, so the rules between cells are single whichever way they wrap.</li>
					<li><strong>It sizes itself by container</strong>: side by side from 56rem of its own width, stacked below — so it is right in a narrow column as well as full-bleed.</li>
					<li><code>--im-statband-pad</code> and <code>--im-statband-split</code> are its two knobs.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</article>
	</div>
